WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.760
You'd think there should be a cure
for the common cold.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.880
Scientists have been working on
cold viruses for over 70 years,

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.840
but they've never found
an effective cure.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.200
The cold is the world's most
prevalent infectious disease,

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.200
adults get between two and four
colds a year and children get

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.320
way, way more.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.960
So this means that you'll probably
get over 200 colds in your lifetime.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.400
And at any one point, hundreds of
millions of people around the world

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.520
have a cold.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.240
So we're going to talk about why
it's so difficult to cure the cold

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.680
in a moment. But before that,
we should go back to

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.800
the very first time we thought
we were going to cure it.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.040
The golden age of cold
research starts in 1957,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.920
with a scientist named Winston Price
at Johns Hopkins University.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.240
Price is the first person to ever
isolate a cold virus in a laboratory,

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.360
and this is a really big deal because
while people had described

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.760
the symptoms of a cold for thousands
of years, nobody had ever found

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.240
the agent that causes it.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.160
So Price becomes sort of like a
minor scientific celebrity.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.680
And he then announces that he's
going to create a vaccine for it.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.120
But, the vaccine doesn’t work
very well, it only protects against

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.760
a very, very small proportion of
infections and Price has no idea why.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.880
Now this is pretty disappointing,
but it's good to think about

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.640
what's going on
in science at the time.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.440
People are incredibly, incredibly
optimistic about the potential for

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.320
research and especially
medical research.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.200
The polio vaccine had just
been created in 1955,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.680
and it's the dawn of
the age of antibiotics.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.480
And after the Second World War,
governments are really, really,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.840
keen to invest in ambitious projects
with definite goals

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.560
that solve big problems in society.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.560
And so the cold becomes one of
these things,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.200
and a huge amount of funding
comes down on the problem.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.200
It moves beyond Price's lab
and cold research centres open

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.720
across the entire world.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.720
And my favourite one of these is the
Common Cold Research Unit

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.160
in Salisbury, in England.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.480
So the scientists there put ads
in the local paper,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.840
basically advertising a two week
vacation in the countryside -

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.840
as long as you were willing to be
locked in a drafty corridor or

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.840
to huff bags of cold infected air
and let scientists observe you

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.200
for a couple hours every day.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.640
And so what scientists unpick over
the next 30 years from all of this,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.280
is that the cold isn't caused by one
virus, it's caused by seven different

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.480
viral families, and each one has
subtypes which are called "serotypes"

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.680
which are recognised differently
by the immune system.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.640
And so, this is why the cold
is so difficult to cure

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.880
and it's why Price's vaccine never
worked, it was only for the one

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.440
cold family and one serotype
he'd isolated.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.360
The problem is, at the time that
this is all known in the 1980s,

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.800
vaccine technology could only fit in
a few serotypes per injection,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.920
without causing an enormous
immune response.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.880
And so, you basically have the
science that we know at an impasse

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.560
with the technology at the time.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.560
And people have been working on
colds for almost a generation,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.800
25 or 30 years, and the scientific
imagination has a tendency to wander.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.920
Elite virologists and government
funding

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.040
start
moving more into other projects,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.240
like AIDS
- which is incredibly deadly

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.040
and almost a total mystery
at the time.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.120
And so colds kind of go out of favour
as a topic of research

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.960
and the common cold unit
even shutters in 1990.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.240
Since then, we've gotten way better
at dealing with infectious diseases.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.880
We've eradicated smallpox,
gotten measles, mumps, rubella,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.600
tetanus, whooping cough,
all kinds of things under control.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.560
And so cold research has undergone
a bit of a renaissance lately.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.480
There's two totally different
approaches that are both

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:12.480
quite promising.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.920
In the UK, there's a group that's
taking a look at every single

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.960
cold serotype, and trying to find a
piece in common that they can target

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.200
a vaccine to.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.480
It's quite like an elegant,
direct approach.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.880
And then there's an American
company who's trying to create

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.120
a super vaccine.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.720
So this would be one shot,
with all 50 of the most common cold

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.040
serotypes in it.
It’s sort of a brute force approach.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.120
But, despite the promise of all this
research, there's still one big

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.520
hurdle to getting it finished
and that has more to do with the

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.520
business of science
than science itself.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:40.640
The thing is,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.960
vaccines aren’t very good bets
for pharmaceutical companies.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.120
They're incredibly
expensive to develop

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.160
and failure rates are really high,
even late in trials.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.800
It takes about $1 billion American
dollars and almost a decade

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.520
to develop a successful vaccine.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.920
Of all the thousands of
pharmaceutical companies that

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.160
there are, only about five work on
vaccines really seriously

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.080
and these are the biggest
and richest companies.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.240
And when it comes down to it, would
you really get an expensive injection

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.440
just to avoid a few days discomfort?
I probably wouldn’t.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.680
So, even after all the progress
over the past 70 years

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.760
we'll probably continue
to suffer from colds.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.920
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.960
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.920
See you again soon!

